## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about client-side privacy, offline UX, and cost trade-offs without server trust. The candidate must specify concrete numbers, crypto choices, and go/no-go criteria, balancing performance, storage, and regulatory costs.

## Key Concepts

- Client-side encryption and key management
- Data growth and local cache sizing
- Delta synchronization vs full sync
- Privacy/compliance implications and auditability

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example using Web Crypto API AES-GCM
async function encrypt(plaintext, cryptoKey) {
  const iv = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(12));
  const encrypted = await crypto.subtle.encrypt(
    { name: 'AES-GCM', iv },
    cryptoKey,
    new TextEncoder().encode(plaintext)
  );
  return { encrypted, iv };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to reason about lightweight on-device features under real-world constraints, including energy budgets, privacy regulations, and user consent requirements.

## Key Concepts
- Cost-benefit analysis under resource constraints
- On-device caching strategies and cache invalidation
- Energy-aware prefetching policies
- Privacy compliance and consent overhead
- Phased rollout strategies and success metrics

## Code Example

```javascript
const PREFETCH_CONFIG = {
  batteryThreshold: 0.25,
  network: 'wifi',
  cacheBudgetMB: 75,
  maxMemoryPercent: 15,
  retryIntervalMs: 30 * 60 * 1000
};

class AssetPrefetcher {
  constructor(config = PREFETCH_CONFIG) {
    this.config = config;
    this.cache = new Map();
  }

  async shouldPrefetch(deviceState) {
    const { batteryLevel, networkType, isCharging } = deviceState;
    return (
      batteryLevel > this.config.batteryThreshold &&
      (networkType === 'wifi' || isCharging)
    );
  }

  async prefetch(assets) {
    if (!(await this.shouldPrefetch(getDeviceState()))) return;
    for (const asset of assets) {
      const cached = await caches.match(asset.url);
      if (!cached) {
        const response = await fetch(asset.url);
        await caches.put(asset.url, response.clone());
      }
    }
  }

  async getAsset(url) {
    const cached = await caches.match(url);
    if (cached) return cached;
    return this.handleMiss(url);
  }

  async handleMiss(url) {
    return fetch(url).catch(() => getOfflineFallback(url));
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical CBA for a local-first search feature, balancing storage, CPU, and privacy.

## Key Concepts
- On-device indexing
- Delta sync costs
- Privacy/compliance
- Update cadence and go/no-go criteria

## Code Example
```javascript
function indexRepo(files) {
  // naive tokenization
  const index = {};
  for (const f of files) {
    const tokens = f.content.split(/\\W+/).filter(Boolean);
    tokens.forEach(t => (index[t] ||= []).push(f.path));
  }
  return index;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure user time saved and energy impact to validate the break-even?
- How would you design for monorepos and very large repos with incremental indexing?

## Why This Is Asked

A practical UX feature tests offline resilience, local storage usage, and simple cross-tab conflict handling without server changes.

## Key Concepts

- Debounce autosave
- localStorage vs IndexedDB
- Versioning for conflict detection
- Cross-tab synchronization basics

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal autosave scaffold
let timer;
function onChange(content){
  clearTimeout(timer);
  timer = setTimeout(()=> {
     const note = { content, lastSaved: Date.now(), version: (NOTE_VERSION++) };
     localStorage.setItem('noteDraft', JSON.stringify(note));
  }, 300);
}
```

